  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nModels continue to heavily suggest that more rain opportunities and \nslightly less hot temperatures are in store for the middle of the \nweek, but don't get your hopes up for a widespread drought buster or \ntrue cool down. The region will still be solidly dominated by high \npressure through Tuesday afternoon, with triple digit heat holding \nsteady. This pattern is set to evolve though, as long range data \nshows troughing over the Great Lakes region sagging southward \nTuesday night through late week, which would suppress high \npressure further southwest over El Paso. This synoptic pattern \nwould allow northwest flow aloft to provide better disturbances \nand weak fronts for the region, especially for the northern \ncombined Panhandles. Ensembles mostly agree this would increase \nrain chances each evening-overnight Tue-Thu. Despite favorable \ndeep layer moisture (PWATs 1.0-1.5\"), most members suggest \nrainfall totals wouldn't be terribly impressive (mostly 0.50\" or \nless any given night). Temperatures are progged to decline to the \n90s Wed-Thu, but not for long as widespread triple digits are \nfavored to resume through the end of the week.\n\nThe system Tuesday night is projected to be the strongest of the \nweek and have abundant moisture in tow. Precipitation probabilities \ncontinue to rise for the entire Panhandles (30-70%), with medium \nrange models now hinting that an organized MCS could push over much \nof the region from northwest to southeast. Some discrepancies exist \namong models, but forecast 20-30kt 0-6km shear along with 1000-2000 \nJ/kg MUCAPE generally spell low-end potential for severe wind gusts \nwith any organized storms Tuesday night. Although the aforementioned \ntrend of lighter rainfall is still the more likely outcome, models \nsuch as the RRFS are placing swaths of much heavier rainfall \nwherever this hypothetical line of storms ultimately passes. If you \ndon't receive any rain Tue night, Wed night is the next best \nopportunity with only slightly lower probabilities of precipitation \n(20-60%).\n\nHarrel",
